// Threat intelligence: STIX 2.1, MITRE ATT&CK 20.1 and MITRE D3FEND 1.6.0: the standards' constraints, their sample data and the links between them
//
// Generated by ../../crosswalk.py from crosswalk.json, sample.json and the model.json and sample.json of each
// standard. Edit those and regenerate, rather than this file.
//
// Each standard's labels and relationship types are prefixed: STIX 2.1 as stix__, MITRE ATT&CK 20.1 as attack__,
// MITRE D3FEND 1.6.0 as d3fend__. The links between them are not.
//
// Operation Quiet Invoice, the STIX bundle's fictional phishing campaign against a UK building society, read through
// ATT&CK and D3FEND. The bundle's four attack patterns and the Rclone tool are ATT&CK's own objects, the same by STIX
// id; the techniques it lacks in the ATT&CK sample, Phishing, Spearphishing Attachment, User Execution, Malicious
// File, Exfiltration Over Web Service and Exfiltration to Cloud Storage, are added from ATT&CK Enterprise 20.1 with
// their tactics, Rclone, and the mitigations Restrict Web-Based Content and Execution Prevention. Those techniques,
// tactics and mitigations are the same as D3FEND's, by ATT&CK id and name, which leads to the defensive techniques
// D3FEND relates to them; and the email, attachment, DLL, URL, processes and traffic the bundle observed are
// instances of the D3FEND digital artifacts that the defensive techniques analyse, filter or isolate. STIX 2.1: A
// threat intelligence bundle on Operation Quiet Invoice, a fictional phishing campaign against a UK building society.
// It holds the threat actor, intrusion set and campaign; the real ATT&CK attack patterns and Rclone tool they use;
// the InvoiceDrop dropper and LedgerRAT implant with their command and control infrastructure; the email, files,
// processes, domain, addresses and network traffic observed on the victim's network; and the indicators, sighting,
// sandbox analysis, courses of action, opinion and TLP:AMBER report around them. Names, domains (example.*) and
// addresses (203.0.113.0/24, AS64500) are reserved for documentation; hashes are illustrative. MITRE ATT&CK 20.1:
// APT29 and the SolarWinds Compromise (C0024) attributed to it, as ATT&CK Enterprise 20.1 records them: the SUNSPOT,
// SUNBURST, TEARDROP and Raindrop malware and the AdFind and Mimikatz tools, the techniques they use, from Compromise
// Software Supply Chain to forged SAML tokens and DCSync, with their parent techniques and tactics. Mitigations of
// those techniques, and the detection strategies for four of them with their Windows analytics and the data
// components they read, complete the picture. Every object, identifier and link is taken from the ATT&CK 20.1
// Enterprise bundle. MITRE D3FEND 1.6.0: Phishing seen through D3FEND 1.6.0: the ATT&CK techniques Spearphishing
// Attachment and Link and the User Execution that follows, the email, URL, mail traffic and executable file artifacts
// they produce, access or execute, and the D3FEND defensive techniques that analyse, filter or block those artifacts,
// such as Sender Reputation Analysis, Homoglyph Detection, URL Analysis, Email Filtering, Dynamic Analysis and
// Executable Allowlisting. Each defensive technique has a reference from its knowledge base article, the ATT&CK
// mitigations D3FEND relates them to are included, as are the NIST SP 800-53 controls mapped to Dynamic Analysis.
// Every class, individual and link is taken from the D3FEND 1.6.0 knowledge graph.
//
// Run it against an empty database of Neo4j 5 or later, Enterprise Edition, for its node key and property existence
// constraints, e.g. with cypher-shell -f model.cypher. It merges every node and relationship, so it can be run again.

// The standards: their constraints

